Laws of Forgiveness of Sins - the Cancellation of Our Guilt

laws of forgiveness of sins


Why Liberation from Guilt Requires Our Participation


For most people who still deal with it, the subject of forgiveness of sins is a difficult, often emotionally stressful and mostly unresolved question. Many Christians will also be surprised or even irritated that forgiveness of sins is linked to laws . They tend to see it as an act of "grace" that God "grant". That is not even wrong, but it raises the question of how it can be done fairly and how arbitrariness can be avoided.

In our culture, in the various Christian religious communities, we are dealing with a clear majority opinion in one respect: through Christ's death on the cross, we are supposedly redeemed from our sins . But if we go into even a little more detail, the similarities quickly cease and contradictions emerge. Last but not least, many Christians, even after the "forgiveness of sins" according to the rites of their religion, continue to struggle with doubts, pangs of conscience and their own dark sides, and they feel anything but truly liberated . Are these doubts about Christ's redemption of sins due to a lack of faith, or is there perhaps a valid reason for them? Is there a reliable forgiveness of sins? Is it tied to certain conditions that we have to fulfill? And can we perceive in any way that this forgiveness of sins has actually taken place?  


Let us first look at some important views of different denominations regarding the forgiveness of sins. They bring us to the problem that, upon closer inspection, there are by no means uniform views between denominations.


The following statement can be found for the Catholic Church:


"Baptism is the first and most significant sacrament of the forgiveness of sins. It unites believers with Christ, who died for the sins of mankind and was raised for their redemption so that they can "live as new men" (Rom 6:4). Through baptism, mankind's sins are now "buried" with water - both personal sins and the original sin imposed on humanity by Adam's disobedience. This leaves nothing that would "prevent the baptized from entering the Kingdom of God", as stated in the Catechism of the Catholic Church (CCC 1263)." (Source: https://www.katholisch.de/artikel/1808-schuld-und-vergebung)


The New Apostolic Church also emphasizes the forgiveness of sins through Christ's death on the cross and adds a condition:


"In order to obtain forgiveness of sins, faith in Jesus Christ as the Savior is a prerequisite." (Source: http://www.nak-erlangen-wuerzburg.de/woran_wir_glauben/suendenvergebung)


The Evangelical Church (EKD) takes the following position:


"Christians believe that Jesus Christ's death on the cross was a punishment he did not deserve. It was not his sin, but that of others that brought him to the cross. Christians say: "Christ died for our sins" - and this vicarious suffering is the starting point for God's reconciliation with mankind. A Christian says: "Only by trusting that Christ is willing to give himself up for me and that I no longer have to prove myself can I escape the power of sin. Faith frees us from self-centeredness."


"Above all, however, the criticism of Jesus' sacrificial death overlooks a crucial point. For in Jesus' death on the cross, God does not sacrifice another to satisfy his thirst for revenge, but in Jesus Christ he gives himself up to reconcile people. Violence does not come from God. Rather, God himself becomes a victim and surrenders himself to human hostility and hatred. The goal is not to legitimize violence, but to overcome it through love and forgiveness."


"[ ... ] God does not leave the sin of mankind, and all the injustice that is connected with it, unpunished. But he says: I take the punishment upon myself. Thus his justice flows into immeasurable love that frees us from injustice and guilt." (Source: https://www.ekd.de/Sunde-11267.htm)



At the end of our collection of quotes, let us add one more aspect that can be found on the homepage "Active Christianity":


"It can also be that we are plagued by our conscience and feel guilty even though we have actually been forgiven. Where does this come from?

 

The devil makes us feel guilty

 

We have an adversary, the devil, who prowls around like a roaring lion, seeking whom he may devour. (1 Peter 5:8) He sows doubts about everything that has to do with the kingdom of God. He is the old serpent and accuser of our brothers. He is a tormenting spirit, and even though one has put one's affairs in order, it is never enough."



The Christian Bible is the most widely printed and most widely translated written work in the world.

It is the basis of numerous denominations. However, its interpretation is just as diverse and by no means uniform.



Questions to the various teachings


It is understandable that different religious communities have different views, otherwise they would not have had to split up but could have remained under one roof. Nevertheless, people who are seriously searching naturally ask themselves the crucial question: Shouldn't all views be compatible with one another if they are to reflect the truth or at least come close to it? For don't we feel very clearly deep within ourselves that real truth must be unified, indivisible and free from contradiction?


And the above statements will automatically give rise to new questions of understanding if one does not want to believe without question and unconsciously. Let us go back to them and look at them from the perspective of the five great laws of creation. These determine and control everything that happens, from the smallest to the largest process; they are perfect in themselves because they are the expression of God's will . If a statement contradicts these central laws, it must be considered incorrect . Thinking, feeling, sensing and becoming aware of their effects is therefore the straight and direct path to understanding God's will, which is in itself absolutely consistent and logical.


The laws of creation are the direct expression of God’s will.

Getting to know and taking into account these self-acting forces is therefore the most direct, clearest and simplest path to the Creator.

It is open to every seriously seeking person of all denominations and also to atheists!


Let's look at the Catholic perspective first. I would like to say in advance that I am not trying to denigrate other religions. However, since these form the basis for hundreds of millions of people, there is a valid reason and also a general interest in asking necessary questions. I see this as permissible; without consciously penetrating important questions of faith, no faith can come alive in us . That would contradict the creative law of movement!


"Baptism is the first and most significant sacrament of the forgiveness of sins."


This raises the question of why a baby just a few days old needs to be forgiven for his sins. Since the churches reject any form of reincarnation from the outset, he cannot have sinned at all and therefore does not need forgiveness. When it comes to original sin: Even if you look at it in such a small way that a long time ago a man named Adam disobeyed God - the law of interaction, i.e. the law of cause, effect and reaction, has only to do with the person who caused the sin , and the consequences can, by the nature of things, only affect the person who brought the cause into the world, in this case Adam. Transferring this to collective guilt would be unjust and therefore not something we can expect of the perfect God! In reality, however, this view is too small anyway and everything is meant differently. Original sin also has a much broader, more natural meaning. Likewise, the rejection of reincarnation does not correspond to reality, which is what recent findings suggest. But all of this would go beyond the scope of this article today; and much of it can be read elsewhere on this website.


"It unites believers with Christ, who died for the sins of men and was raised for their redemption so that they can "live as new men""


Here comes the central point that runs through all Christian religions: the interpretation of Jesus' death on the cross as a redemptive death for humanity. But why this is not possible according to the law of creation is something I have explained in detail in my comments on Good Friday . Of course, such a redemption would also violate the principle of causation , which is inseparably embedded in both the law of interaction and the creation law of compensation . Jesus cannot therefore take on our sins at all, since he was not the cause. If an exception were possible, the perfect law of interaction would have to be changed ; but that would be proof of his imperfection, which is out of the question. And since we are supposed to gain a better understanding by experiencing the consequences of our sins so that we do not commit them again, a convenient redemption by someone else would also violate the necessary creation law of movement - in other words, three of the five laws.


If we ask ourselves further whether the sentence: "... so that they can live as new people" has become reality, then in view of the current conditions on this planet, most people - including Christians - are likely to have legitimate doubts, just as they do with the statement: "It unites the believers with Christ, ..."


Finally, the sentence: " There remains nothing that would hinder the baptized from entering the Kingdom of God, as stated in the Catechism of the Catholic Church (CCC 1263)."


This also raises questions. What happens if an infant dies before baptism? And wouldn't an infant be privileged above all other people if it were granted the grace to die shortly after baptism so that it could then immediately enter paradise, the kingdom of eternal joy? Moreover, does this grace of complete forgiveness of sins through baptism only come to Catholic children? If so, wouldn't the perfect Creator be very unfair if he allowed billions of people to be born into other cultures that are not Catholic or at least Christian? Do they then experience disadvantages if they are born into Muslim, Jewish, Buddhist, Hindu or other families? In other words: does he arbitrarily favor one denomination from birth, and the others are unfortunately out of luck?


Another point is worth thinking about: Where is the limit of the forgiveness of sins through Christ's death on the cross? Did this also apply to a Nazi leader who may have millions of human lives on his conscience? If not, up to what degree of sin is forgiven, and when is it no longer possible? Or vice versa: Can highly decent people of other faiths not be redeemed if they do not believe in Jesus of Nazareth, and perhaps have never learned about his teachings?



Let us now move on to the statement of the New Apostolic religion: " In order to obtain forgiveness of sins, faith in Jesus Christ as the Savior is a prerequisite."


This is not compatible with the Catholic statement of forgiveness of sins through baptism, since an infant is not capable of this belief. At least this statement is rather plausible, since it presupposes a conscious confrontation with Jesus. But the same problem of salvation through death on the cross also exists here, as does the question already mentioned: Can people of other faiths not be saved if they do not believe in Jesus of Nazareth, perhaps have never learned about his teachings?



Thirdly, the Protestant Church:


"Christians believe that Jesus Christ's death on the cross was a punishment he did not deserve. It was not his sin, but that of others that brought him to the cross. Christians say: "Christ died for our sins" - and this vicarious suffering is the starting point for God's reconciliation with mankind. A Christian says: "Only by trusting that Christ is willing to give himself up for me and that I no longer have to prove myself can I escape the power of sin. Faith frees us from self-centeredness."


The first sentence is absolutely correct, and the second can also be left as it is, albeit in a different context! But to derive reconciliation from the crucifixion, wouldn't that contradict God's own 5th commandment: "You shall not kill!"? And isn't Christ's "devotion" to be understood as meaning that he died for his own teachings, since fleeing from his persecutors would have raised doubts among the people who trusted him ? Then there are questions of logic: What does it mean to no longer have to prove yourself? Where do we find "can I escape the power of sin" in reality? And does n't the belief that God allows himself to be killed for people promote self-centeredness and hubris, to be particularly valuable , instead of overcoming them?


Let’s go further:


"Above all, however, the criticism of Jesus' sacrificial death overlooks a crucial point. For in Jesus' death on the cross, God does not sacrifice another to satisfy his thirst for revenge, but in Jesus Christ he gives himself up to reconcile people. Violence does not come from God. Rather, God himself becomes a victim and surrenders himself to human hostility and hatred. The goal is not to legitimize violence, but to overcome it through love and forgiveness."


So does God supposedly give himself up "to reconcile people"? Wouldn't that be the task of people themselves? Are we humans so valuable that God should suffer for us? Doesn't this convey an incredibly weak image of God, which is really not acceptable for the Creator and Ruler of all worlds?


"God does not leave the sin of mankind, and all the injustice that is connected with it, unpunished. But he says: I take the punishment upon myself. Thus his justice flows into immeasurable love that frees us from injustice and guilt."


That would be a violation of the polluter pays principle, i.e. a violation of the creation law of interaction! He himself takes the punishment for the sins of mankind, and that is supposed to fulfill justice? God suffers so that mankind can continue to sin with impunity? All of that is incompatible with all of the creation laws!


And because all of this is extremely difficult for a seriously searching person to believe, he also suffers from these things! So it often happens that he actually experiences what is mentioned on the "Active Christianity" page: "It can also be that we are plagued by our conscience and feel guilty, even though we have actually been forgiven. Where does that come from?" However, the convenient conclusion: "The devil makes us feel guilty" is then immediately included, which immediately finds the scapegoat. But we will soon see that this is absolutely not the case, but that the pangs of conscience actually come from our inner voice, which is warning us and trying to bring us back on the right path! -


After all these digressions, let us now look at the topic of forgiveness of sins in a completely different way:



Is God "natural" and understandable to us?


This question can be answered with "yes" without a doubt and is easily justified! Because nature, which is the basis of our existence, a place where we feel good and "recharge our batteries", and which also helps us to resolve difficult questions and emotional problems more easily, was created by God in its simple and touching beauty - at least that is how every religious person sees it.


The beauty of nature: is it not in itself a proof of the naturalness of God who created it?


Furthermore, our spirit - and by this we mean our spiritual core, our actual "I" - is the truth principle within us. If it were not buried, which unfortunately is more or less the case with all of us, it would have contact with God's will in its simple naturalness and would be able to understand it. But that also means:


The truth will always seem natural to us in the depths of our soul , because naturalness and truth are inseparable!


But if we look at the alleged salvation of mankind through Christ's death on the cross, we see that this goes against all common sense! We would consider any person who would seriously consider the painful sacrifice of a completely innocent person in order to allow the guilty to get away with absolutely no punishment as a candidate for psychiatric care. However, hundreds of millions of people can say such things about God ! And in order for this absurdity to be accepted, God must be "exalted" to the point of being unnatural ! He is then "so full of grace that it exceeds human comprehension". His love is so "boundless that he even sacrifices his son to save us poor sinners".


As if there were no other cause and no other ways!


